<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-papers.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-papers-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-papers.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Line-by-Line Estimate Audit</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Insurance Estimate Review in New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">Before you accept a check, file a supplement or invoke appraisal, it helps to know exactly where the carrier’s estimate falls short. We review it line by line and tell you plainly what we find.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>Deliverable</small><b>Written review</b></li><li><small>Covers</small><b>Scope, pricing, depreciation</b></li><li><small>Good first step</small><b>Before appraisal</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>A second set of eyes before anything is signed</h2>
<p>Carrier estimates are usually produced in specialized software, organized by room and trade, and printed as dozens of pages of line items with codes and abbreviations. Most policyholders don’t have the background to check whether the right items are there, priced correctly and depreciated fairly. That’s where a review comes in.</p>
<p>An estimate review from New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser is a standalone service. It doesn’t commit you to appraisal or anything else. Sometimes the review shows the estimate is reasonable, and we’ll say so. Sometimes it shows a handful of missed items that a supplement request can fix. Sometimes the gap is large enough that appraisal makes sense.</p>
<h2>Where an audit digs in</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Questions we ask</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Are all the damaged areas included? Are adjacent and hidden areas addressed?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Measurements</td><td>Do the square footage, roof squares and wall heights match the building?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Are unit costs current for your region? Are minimum charges and trip fees reasonable?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Matching</td><td>Does the repair leave mismatched surfaces? What does the policy say?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code items</td><td>Are permit-driven requirements included or explained?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overhead &amp; profit</td><td>Is a general contractor reasonably needed for this job?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Is it applied consistently, and to labor as well as materials?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Why a Manchester number isn’t a Colebrook number</h2>
<p>Construction costs vary across New Hampshire. Labor in the southern tier is shaped by competition with larger metro markets to the south; in the Great North Woods, there are fewer contractors and long drives to the job. Seasonal demand in the Lakes Region and ski towns drives prices up at certain times of year. A carrier estimate that applies the same pricing to a Nashua condo and a Pittsburg camp may miss real costs in one or both places.</p>
<p>Overhead and profit deserve their own look. When a repair needs a roofer, an electrician, a drywall crew and a painter, coordinating them is real work, and a general contractor is often reasonably needed. Estimates sometimes leave overhead and profit off entirely, or apply it to some trades and not others, without explaining why.</p>
<h2>Reading the depreciation column</h2>
<p>Depreciation is where many estimates quietly lose value. Common issues include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Depreciating labor on items where your policy may not allow it — something to check closely in your form.</li>
<li>Using an age for the roof or flooring that doesn’t match the records.</li>
<li>Applying high depreciation to items with long useful lives.</li>
<li>Withholding depreciation without explaining how to recover it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/depreciation-holdback-planner/">depreciation holdback planner</a> can help you track what has been withheld and what you may be able to recover after repairs.</p>
<h2>The audit report you get back</h2>
<ol>
<li>A written summary of the main issues, in plain language.</li>
<li>A list of specific line items that are missing, under-measured or under-priced.</li>
<li>Where useful, a comparison estimate built from our own inspection.</li>
<li>Our honest view on next steps: accept, supplement or consider appraisal.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Not legal advice</b>A review looks at the estimate, not at whether the insurer handled your claim lawfully. If you believe the insurer has misrepresented the policy or delayed unreasonably, you can contact the New Hampshire Insurance Department or speak with an attorney.</div>
<p>To start, send us the estimate and policy through our <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact page</a>. You can also score your own file with the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/nh-appraisal-readiness-score/">appraisal readiness score</a>.</p>

<h2>What owners ask about this</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I need an inspection for an estimate review?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not always. Some reviews can be done from the estimate, photos and documents. For larger or more complex claims, an on-site inspection usually produces a more reliable result.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I use your review to request a supplement?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Many policyholders send the findings to their adjuster as a supplement request, which sometimes resolves the dispute without appraisal.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the review shows the estimate is fair?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Then we’ll tell you so. Knowing the estimate holds up can save you the time and cost of a dispute.</p></div></details></div>
